03

1. Link to another webpage (external or internal)
<a href="https://example.com">Visit Example</a>
<a href="about.html">About Us</a>
- href specifies the URL.
- Opens the linked page when clicked.

2. Link to a section within the same page (fragment identifier)
<a href="#section2">Go to Section 2</a>
<h2 id="section2">Section 2</h2>
- Scrolls to an element with the matching id.

3. Link to email (mailto)
<a href="mailto:someone@example.com">Send Email</a>
- Opens the default email client to send an email.

4. Link to phone number (tel)
<a href="tel:+1234567890">Call Us</a>
- Initiates a phone call on devices that support it.

5. Download a file
<a href="file.pdf" download>Download PDF</a>
- The download attribute tells the browser to download the file instead of opening it.

6. Open link in a new tab
<a href="https://example.com" target="_blank">Open in New Tab</a>
- target="_blank" opens the link in a new browser tab or window.

7. Link with relationship or metadata
<a href="https://example.com" rel="nofollow">External Link</a>
- rel specifies the relationship between the current page and the linked page.
	- Examples: nofollow, noopener, author, external.

8. Link with download filename
<a href="file.pdf" download="NewName.pdf">Download PDF</a>
- Lets you specify the name of the downloaded file.

9. JavaScript actions (rare, usually for interactivity)
<a href="#" onclick="alert('Hello!')">Click Me</a>
- Executes JavaScript when the link is clicked.
- Best practice: Use <button> for actions instead of anchor links.

10. Link to a specific protocol
<a href="ftp://example.com/file.zip">FTP Link</a>
<a href="sms:+1234567890">Send SMS</a>
- Supports protocols like ftp, sms, tel, mailto.
